<Page title="Editable Video with Code — The EditableFrame Guide" description="Learn how EditableFrame connects creative code, scene-specific controls and reversible edits. Explore the browser playground and the local video SDK beta." path="/guide/">
<article class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">THE GUIDE / BETA 0.1</p><h1>Creative code.<br/><em>Editable details.</em></h1><p>EditableFrame is a framework for videos created with code and refined through human or agent edits. A scene keeps its creative source while exposing optional controls for the details people want to change. New libraries can provide new ways to draw; the controls do not define a fixed visual language.</p><h2>Try a scene in your browser</h2><ol><li>Choose a study in <a href="/#gallery">the collection</a> and press Play.</li><li>Change its colors, geometry, camera or timing with the panel beside the scene.</li><li>Pause and scrub to compare the same moment. Undo and redo let you try a different direction.</li><li>Save a PNG frame or a JSON edit recipe. Open that edit recipe here to restore its settings.</li></ol><p>The browser saves edits locally when storage is available. Settings are not uploaded to Stitchable. Clearing browser data removes that local copy, so download settings you want to keep. A downloaded edit recipe is specific to this playground runtime; it is not an MP4 or a portable SDK project.</p><h2>What does the local SDK do?</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Here in the playground</th><th>In supported local SDK projects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Play and edit 12 editable studies</td><td>Author clips and optional controls with code</td></tr><tr><td>Undo/redo and browser-local saves</td><td>Persist revision-checked CLI and preview edits</td></tr><tr><td>Download PNG frames and edit settings</td><td>Export MP4 with audio, transparent ProRes 4444 MOV, or PNG/WAV sequences</td></tr><tr><td>Curated source running in your browser</td><td>Trusted local JavaScript, not an untrusted-code sandbox</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The SDK beta includes typed anchors, keyframes, media ports, asset hashes, generated panels and reversible commands. The film studies on this site are not all packaged as generic CLI export templates. Production streaming decode, general HTML-to-pixel capture and complete host-app integration remain further work.</p><h2>How do code and controls connect?</h2><p>The source defines the drawing or scene. Stable anchor IDs identify editable details, and a schema defines valid property values. People and agents send validated changes through the same history. A 3D clip can expose a camera and materials; an explainer can expose labels and signal speed. Controls are optional.</p><h2>Can an AI agent use EditableFrame?</h2><p>The local framework provides a model-neutral CLI and a shared SKILL.md. Claude Code, Codex and other tool-capable agents can use those entry points, with host-specific installation. The framework itself does not require a model API key or a Stitchable account. Integration with a particular agent host should be tested independently.</p><p>The MIT source is available on <a href="https://github.com/Stitchable-ai/editable-frames" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>, with local setup, agent skills and the beta contract. Install from a source checkout; an npm package is not published.</p><h2>Who is behind it?</h2><p>EditableFrame is built by parent company <a href="https://stitchable.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stitchable</a>. For a complete video-editing application, explore <a href="https://stitchable.ai/studio/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stitchable Pro Studio</a>. The framework is an independent project with its own beta contract.</p><p><a href="/credits/">Read the credits and licenses</a>, or <a href="/genres/castle/">start changing a little 3D world</a>.</p></article>
</Page>
